IoT Flood Monitoring System — Field Deployment Guide


This document describes the end-to-end process for deploying a LoRaWAN-based flood monitoring node in the field, from bench testing through The Things Network (TTN) configuration and on-site troubleshooting.
Hardware Used
Gateways
•	Milesight UG67
•	RAK7289C V2
Sensors
•	Dragino (water level, model TBD) 
•	Milesight EM410-RDL
•	Daviteq WS-LRW-RLC-01
Deployment Sites
•	Juving Chirrdi Khola Bridge
•	Kaya Khet
Network Platform
•	The Things Network (TTN) / The Things Stack



________________________________________
1. Bench Setup & Sandbox Testing
Before any field deployment, validate the hardware on the bench:
1.	Power on the gateway (Milesight UG67 or RAK7289C V2) and connect it to a network with internet access.
2.	Register the gateway in TTN under a sandbox/test application, separate from the production application, so field data isn't mixed with test data.
3.	Power on the sensor(s) to be deployed (Dragino / Milesight EM410-RDL / Daviteq WS-LRW-RLC-01).
4.	Register the sensor as an end device in the same sandbox TTN application.
5.	Confirm the gateway forwards uplinks from the sensor and that TTN shows live data in the Live Data tab.
6.	Only proceed to field deployment once this bench test is stable.


________________________________________
2. Site Survey & Pre-Deployment Requirements
At the physical deployment location, confirm the following before installing anything permanently:
•	Power availability at or near the site (grid power, or plan for solar).
•	Internet connectivity at the gateway location (cellular/WiFi backhaul depending on gateway config).
•	Cabling needs: 
o	CAT6 cable for short-distance Ethernet runs.
o	Fibre optic cable if the run between components exceeds 50 meters.


________________________________________
3. Field Installation Process
3.1 Signal / Link Testing (before fixing anything permanently)
1.	Place the gateway and sensor at their intended provisional positions.
2.	Power on both devices.
3.	Check TTN Live Data to confirm the gateway is receiving uplinks from the sensor.
4.	If uplinks are not received reliably: 
o	Reposition the sensor and/or gateway.
o	Prefer a line-of-sight path between the sensor and gateway with no obstructions.
o	Repeat testing after each reposition until a stable, consistent connection is achieved.
5.	Only once uplinks are consistently received should the devices be permanently fixed in place.
3.2 Permanent Mounting
1.	Fix the gateway and sensor at the validated positions.
2.	Weatherproof both units: 
o	Protect from rain and direct weather exposure (enclosures, weatherproof housings, cable glands, etc.).
3.3 Power System
1.	To avoid power outages, use a solar panel + battery backup system.
2.	Select the battery type based on the site's climate — batteries degrade faster under temperature extremes, so choose a battery rated for the expected weather conditions at the site (e.g., temperature-tolerant sealed lead-acid or equivalent).
3.	Mount the battery and charge controller/power unit off the ground on a stable platform, with power cables (red/black) run neatly along the wall and routed away from standing water or direct rain exposure.
Reference: See deployment photo — battery placed on an elevated crate/platform, connected via a charge controller box, with wall-routed cabling to keep the connection point protected.


________________________________________
4. Things Network (TTN) Configuration
Once the gateway and sensor are physically installed and stable:
1.	Log in to The Things Network / The Things Stack console.
2.	Move (or re-register) the gateway and sensor from the sandbox application into the production application for the deployment site (e.g., a per-site application such as juving-chirrdi-khola-bridge or kaya-khet).
3.	Configure the end device: 
o	Dev EUI / App Key / App EUI: using placeholder values for now — replace with the actual device credentials before going live. 
o	DevEUI: <DEV_EUI>AppEUI: <APP_EUI>AppKey: <APP_KEY>
o	Payload Formatter: TODO — to be added. Paste the sensor-specific decoder (Dragino / Milesight EM410-RDL / Daviteq WS-LRW-RLC-01) under the device's Payload Formatters tab in TTN.
o	TDC (Transmit Data Cycle / uplink interval): TODO — set according to site power budget and required monitoring frequency. Confirm value before final deployment.
4.	Save configuration and confirm the device shows as connected/active.


________________________________________
5. Final Testing
1.	Confirm uplinks are arriving consistently in TTN's Live Data for the production application.
2.	Confirm the payload formatter correctly decodes raw payloads into readable sensor values (water level, etc.).
3.	Confirm the configured TDC interval matches actual observed uplink frequency.
4.	Leave the system running for a soak-test period and re-check for dropped uplinks before considering the deployment complete.

